<file path="_includes/ability.md">
## The Ability

Cave Fangs allows traps to be placed across the battlefield. The Stalactites variant grants the ability to place them as a free action. Placing a trap directly on an enemy during combat triggers it immediately.

### Names used in this guide

| Name | What it is |
|---|---|
| **Cave Fangs** | The 5th-level conjuration spell, with two variants. |
| **Cave Fangs: Stalactites** | The variant used in this guide. Casting it is a standard action and grants a timed buff. |
| **Stalactites Trap** | The ability granted by the buff, used to place the actual traps. In-game, it is titled "Cave Fangs" with the description: "Create Stalactites trap". |
| **Buff** | Refers to the Cave Fangs: Stalactites buff. |
| **Trap** | Short for Stalactites Trap. |

### The spell and the trap

??? spell "Cave Fangs"
    {% include '_includes/cave_fangs.md' %}

??? spell "Cave Fangs: Stalactites"
    {% include '_includes/cave_fangs_stalactites.md' %}

??? spell "Stalactites Trap"
    {% include '_includes/stalactites_trap.md' %}

Both the spell and the traps it creates count as conjuration spells.

### Trap placement 

Placing a trap is a free action, with no fixed limit on the number placed per turn. The practical limit is the buff's remaining duration, as each trap consumes 10 minutes.

The number of available traps is therefore equal to the buff's remaining duration divided by 10 minutes. Since the buff lasts 10 minutes per caster level, this allows one trap per caster level before modifiers. Effects that directly extend the buff's duration provide additional traps (see [Enduring Spells](#enduring-spells)).

Traps remain on the ground for 1 hour per level, although this rarely matters in combat. They can be placed directly on enemies, triggering immediately.

### Trap spell level

The trap's spell level is **half the character level, capped at 9**. It is independent of Cave Fangs' own spell level (5), the spell slot used, and caster level. Several interactions discussed later in this guide depend on the trap's spell level.

### Stalagmites version

The Stalagmites variant uses a swift action to place a trap, limiting placement to one per turn in combat. This is why the guide focuses on Stalactites.

??? spell "Cave Fangs: Stalagmites"
    {% include '_includes/cave_fangs_stalagmites.md' %}
</file>

<file path="_includes/angel_mythic_6_damage_potential.md">
#### Mythic Rank 6 damage potential

The following calculations estimate the damage from a single **Cave Fangs** cast, with Sword of Heaven active and all 144 traps hitting an (infinite hp) target.

Maximised damage is rounded down by the game for each trap. Average damage retains fractional values until the total is calculated.

**Stalactite Trap damage**

These calculations assume Sword of Heaven is active, including the *Bound of Possibility* buff. The Bolster is not applied to the Sword of Heaven extra dice. 

- Base damage with Bolster: \(3d8 + 6\)
- Damage with Sword of Heaven: \(7d8 + 6\)

Plague of Madness provides Empower and Maximise, while Promise of Greatness provides the Bolster effect. Abolish Guile is another 1.5 times multiplier.

\[
\left\lfloor (7 \times 8 + 6) \times 1.5^2  \right\rfloor
= 139
\]

**Theoretical maximum Trap damage**

This setup assumes:

- Hellknight 10 main character (not recommended)
- Level 20 Paladin ally
- Fire Conversion and Flame Curse on the enemy
- A Guile Demon

\[
\left\lfloor
(7 \times 8 + 6 + 20 + 20) \times 1.5^3
\right\rfloor
= 344
\]

**Geomancy damage**

At character level 18 or higher, Geomancy deals:

\[
9d6 \rightarrow 13d6
\]

The average damage with Abolish Guile is:

\[
13 \times \frac{6 + 1}{2} \times 1.5 = 68.25
\]


**Elemental Barrage damage**

Elemental Barrage deals \(6d6\) damage when an enemy is damaged after also having been damaged during the previous turn.

Witch Abolish Guile:

\[
6 \times \frac{6 + 1}{2} \times 1.5 = 31.5
\]

##### Total single-target damage potential

Using all 144 traps against a Flame Cursed enemy, the theoretical damage consists of:

- 344 maximised trap damage per trap
- 68.25 average Geomancy damage per trap

Elemental Barrage is excluded from this total.

**Total across 144 traps:**

\[
(344 + 68.25) \times 144
= \boxed{59\,364 \text{ average damage}}
\]

Wether Elemental Barrage increases this further (you lose Flame Curse multiplier on half the traps) is left as an excercise for the reader.
</file>

<file path="_includes/dc.md">
### DC

As with regular spells, trap saving throws are affected by two kinds of modifiers: bonuses to DC and penalties to enemy saving throws. For a full list, see the [Bonuses (WotR) page](https://pathfinderkingmaker.fandom.com/wiki/Bonuses_(WotR)#Spell_DC) on the wiki.

Penalties include conditions such as shaken or sickened, Dispel Synergy, Ambuscading Spell and similar effects. These work as expected, just as they do for other spells.

#### Why DC Matters

Cave Fangs traps deal half damage on a successful save, so enemies with high Reflex saves already reduce their damage output considerably. The greater concern is evasion. Against an enemy with evasion and a high Reflex save, traps may deal no damage at all. [Geomancy](#geomancy) still damages these enemies, but the loss in damage remains substantial.

??? class-feature "Evasion"
    {% include '_includes/evasion.md' %}

#### How the DC reaches the traps

All DC bonuses that would normally apply according to their descriptions also apply to the Cave Fangs: Stalactites buff. When the buff is cast, its DC is inherited by the Stalactites Trap ability. These bonuses do not need to apply directly to the trap's DC.

This allows DC and casting-stat items to be equipped for the buff's duration, then replaced with more useful equipment for combat. Heighten, the casting ability score, the Dagger of the Betrayer and the Quarterstaff of the War Mage all work this way.

??? item-weapon "Dagger of the Betrayer"
    {% include '_includes/dagger_of_the_betrayer.md' %}

??? item-weapon "Quarterstaff of the War Mage"
    {% include '_includes/quarterstaff_of_the_war_mage.md' %}

#### The double DC bug

!!! bug "Some DC bonuses apply twice"
    Some DC increases apply to the Cave Fangs buff, as expected, but **also apply directly to the trap itself**.

    - When the buff is cast, the trap inherits its DC. This is normal.
    - While the bonus remains active, it also increases the trap's DC directly, so it is counted a second time.
    - Unequipping the item that provides the bonus leaves only the inherited increase, as with other DC bonuses.
    - Equipping the item after casting means the bonus is not included in the inherited DC, but still increases the trap's DC directly.

The following sources are known to be affected:

??? feat "Spell Focus"
    {% include '_includes/spell_focus.md' %}

??? feat "Greater Spell Focus"
    {% include '_includes/greater_spell_focus.md' %}

??? mythic-feature "Mythic Spell Focus"
    {% include '_includes/mythic_spell_focus.md' %}

??? mythic-feature "Expanded Arsenal"
    {% include '_includes/expanded_arsenal.md' %}

??? item-weapon "Numerian Greatsword"
    {% include '_includes/numerian_greatsword.md' %}

??? item "Robe of the Seven Sins"
    {% include '_includes/robe_of_the_seven_sins.md' %}

??? item "Heartstone of the Night Hag Queen"
    {% include '_includes/heartstone_of_the_night_hag_queen.md' %}

??? mythic-feature "Hell's Decree"
    {% include '_includes/hells_decree.md' %}

    === "Decree of Hunt"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_hunt.md' %}

    === "Decree of Vengeance"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_vengeance.md' %}

    === "Decree of Hell's Fires"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_hells_fires.md' %}

    === "Decree of School Magic"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_school_magic.md' %}

??? demon-feature "Demonic Rage"
    {% include '_includes/demonic_rage.md' %}

Hell's Decree comes from the [Devil](#devil-m8) path.

#### Spell Focus and Expanded Arsenal

The Expanded Arsenal exploit can increase the Spell Focus bonuses to **+16 DC** at Mythic Rank 10, at the cost of all five mythic feats. There are two variations, both covered in detail in the Steam guide *DC magic for dummies: the Art of dominant Crowd Control!* by Raven Paradox. I learned a lot from this guide.

Because of the double DC bug, this provides up to **+32 DC** to the traps.

#### Numerian Greatsword

The Numerian Greatsword appears in the list above, but is awkward to use:

- It has a limited number of charges, which are consumed each round.
- It is a two-handed weapon, so it competes for hand slots with [Plague of Madness](#meta-plague).
- Unequipping it removes all its charges, which only return after resting. Other weapons can be changed without losing their charges.

Without the last limitation, Plague of Madness could be used to buff Cave Fangs before switching to the Numerian Greatsword for its +2 direct DC bonus. However, equipping Plague of Madness to cast the buff also removes the Greatsword's charges.

Titan Fighter's Giant Weapon Wielder offers a solution. It allows a two-handed weapon to be wielded in one hand, making it possible to use Plague of Madness and the Numerian Greatsword together. This also provides the +2 inherited DC bonus, for a total of +4 DC. After casting the buff, Plague of Madness can be replaced with another weapon; the Greatsword must remain equipped.

??? class-feature "Giant Weapon Wielder"
    {% include '_includes/giant_weapon_wielder.md' %}

Once the Greatsword's charges are exhausted, the Quarterstaff of the War Mage can be used to cast the buff instead. This staff only affects the DC of Cave Fangs: Stalactites.
</file>

<file path="_includes/dr_and_incorporeal.md">
### Damage Reduction

Physical spells have a pretty big downside in WotR: they are affected by DR.

Weapon damage has a huge number of ways to bypass (various levels of) DR. 

For energy spell damage, Ascendant Element and the Vulnerability Hex bypass energy resistances and immunities, see [Resistances and immunities](#resistances-and-immunities).

I only know of three ways to bypass DR for physical damage spells:

- [Smite (and Mark of Justice)](#smites)
- Conversion to energy, see [Holy Conversion](#holy-conversion) and [Negative Energy Conversion](#negative-energy-conversion)
- The [Demon's Aspect of Deskari](#demon-m9)


#### How much DR matters

DR matters a lot if you only have the base spell. It deals 3d8, so 3 to 24 damage, but 90% of the time it falls between 7 and 20. That is halved on a successful Reflex save.

Even regular late Act 2 enemies like Vrocks have DR 10, which means that on a successful save all the damage is likely to be mitigated.

Once you have the traps [maximized and empowered](#meta-plague), you deal 36 damage every cast without any further buffs, although those further buffs are hard to come by. Enemies with more than 15 DR are pretty rare. It is a significant damage nerf, but it does not invalidate the ability, and you can bring a Paladin with [Mark of Justice](#mark-of-justice) for select enemies.

### Incorporeal

Incorporeal is a much bigger problem.

??? class-feature "Incorporeal"
    {% include '_includes/trait_incorporeal.md' %}

While the text says weapons, it does in fact include physical damage spells. Incorporeal enemies, for example Shadow Demons, which are relatively common from late Act 3 onwards, are completely immune to the damage of your traps.

I only know of two ways to deal with this:

- The Demon's [Aspect of Deskari](#demon-deskari), which is a mythic rank 9 ability, so just before the end of the game.
- Conversion to energy: the earliest is mythic rank 3 for the [Lich](#lich-m3), then mythic rank 6 for the [Angel](#angel-m6).

Note that converting to negative energy still means incorporeal enemies take only half damage. For holy damage it is full damage.

Incorporeal also gives immunity to Sneak Attacks which matter for [Surprise Spells](#surprise-spells). It can be bypassed trough holy conversion.
</file>

<file path="_includes/elemental_barrage.md">
#### Elemental Barrage

??? mythic-feature "Elemental Barrage"
    {% include '_includes/elemental_barrage.md' %}

Although the description refers to energy damage, the ability requires elemental damage to function. Two different elements are therefore needed. Fire is available through the Geomancer dip and its bloodline; electricity is the easiest second element to obtain.

??? item "Stormlord's Resolve"
    {% include '_includes/stormlords_resolve.md' %}

Despite its description, Stormlord's Resolve does not convert all spell damage to electricity. It affects only energy damage, so energy conversion is required before it can work with Cave Fangs.

Toggling between fire and electricity conversion between casts causes affected enemies to take an additional 1d6 damage per mythic rank.

The main drawback is the mythic feat cost. The following selections are required:

- Elemental Barrage
- Ascendant Element: Fire
- Ascendant Element: Electricity

Vulnerability Curse can replace the latter two selections. See [Resistances and immunities](#resistances-and-immunities).
</file>

<file path="_includes/elemental_conversion.md">
### Elemental Conversion

Once damage has been converted to an energy type, it can be converted again to an elemental type. This affects trap damage, but not Geomancy or its damage to the caster, which remain holy, unholy or negative.

One way to achieve this is through Elemental Bloodline (Fire). Since the build already takes a dip into [Geomancer](#geo-bloodline), access to this bloodline is straightforward.

#### Reasons to convert to fire

- [Flame Curse](#std-hexes), for additional single-target damage.
- [Items](#fire-items) that trigger on fire damage.
- [Elemental Barrage](#elemental-barrage).

#### Resistances and immunities

Many demons have elemental resistances and immunities. Ascendant Element is therefore useful when converting damage to an element.

??? mythic-feature "Ascendant Element"
    {% include '_includes/ascendant_element.md' %}

Ascendant Element is available for acid, cold, electricity, fire, negative energy and sonic damage.

An alternative is the Vulnerability Curse hex. Despite its name, it does not grant vulnerability in the same way as Flame Curse; instead, it bypasses immunities and resistances.

??? hex "Vulnerability Curse"
    {% include '_includes/vulnerability_curse.md' %}

Vulnerability Curse uses a standard action and affects a single target. The traps already have a small area of effect, so the single-target limitation is relatively minor, particularly because Geomancy provides the main source of area damage.
</file>

<file path="_includes/enduring_spells.md">
### Enduring Spells

??? mythic-feature "Enduring Spells"
    {% include '_includes/enduring_spells.md' %}

Enduring Spells extends the Cave Fangs buff to 24 hours. Since each trap consumes 10 minutes of the buff, this allows **144 traps from a single cast**. 

#### Timing 

The first mythic ability becomes available at the end of Act 1. Solo characters gain more XP, so reaching level 10 by this point is only possible when playing solo (see the [Shared Experience Addendum](../SharedXP/wotr_graphs.md)). Soloing until Gray Garrison, without necessarily soloing the location itself, allows level 9 to be reached before Act 2. Either way, Enduring Spells and Cave Fangs can work together almost immediately.

The drawback is that physical DR remains difficult to overcome, while a single trap deals little damage. Enemies with physical DR therefore require many traps to kill. See [DR and Incorporeal](#dr-and-incorporeal).

Mythic Rank 3, reached at the end of Act 2, is another good opportunity to take it. In Act 3, trap damage can be increased through [Plague of Madness](#meta-plague) and the [mythic choice](#mythic-angel). A Paladin can also provide [Mark of Justice](#mark-of-justice) to help overcome DR on bosses.
</file>

