<file path="_includes/intro.md">
## Introduction

Cave Fangs is considered cheesy, mainly because of its interaction with **Enduring Spells**. The Stalactites variant grants a buff that allows traps to be placed as free actions, with each placement consuming 10 minutes of its duration. Extend the buff to 24 hours and a single cast provides up to **144 traps**.

That is a lot of free-action damage from a single spell slot. Thus the ability is already quite strong without any elaborate combinations.

However, some may be surprised by how many things interact with each placement. Every trap counts as a spell cast for Geomancy, works with Surprise Spells, and can benefit from a wide range of damage bonuses. Add metamagic, damage conversion and mythic-path features, and the results can get rather ridiculous.

A Mythic Rank 6 Angel's trap can deal up to [344 damage](#mythic-rank-6-angel-damage-potential). Gold Dragon's Geomancy deals [159 damage](#gd-geo-aoe) to all enemies within 30 feet. Surprise Spells can add 19d6 damage per trap as a Demon, or [15d10](#gd-single) as a Gold Dragon. Across 144 traps, this results in some [extreme theoretical damage figures](#gd-max-dmg).

The guide covers the mechanics, class options, mythic paths and individual interactions in detail. It also discusses potential pitfalls, weaknesses and ways to address them. The table below provides a quick overview, with links to the relevant sections.

### Synergy overview

| Synergy | What it adds |
|---|---|
| [Enduring Spells](#enduring-spells) | Extends the buff to 24 hours, allowing up to **144 free-action trap placements** from one cast.  |
| [Geomancy](#geomancy) | Every trap placement triggers Geomancy. At character level 18 or higher, this can deal 9d6 damage in a 30-foot radius, with no save against the damage. It effectively adds a second area-damage effect to every trap. |
| [Metamagic](#metamagic), [Plague of Madness](#meta-plague), [Promise of Greatness](#meta-greatness), [Perfect Soul](#gd-m10) | *Plague of Madness* provides Empower and Maximise; *Promise of Greatness* provides Bolster. These effects can be applied to the buff so that the traps inherit them. Gold Dragon eventually gets automatic Empower and Maximise through Perfect Soul. |
| [Spell DC](#dc) | The traps inherit the DC of the Cave Fangs buff. Some bonuses also apply directly to the traps, resulting in the double DC bug.  |
| [Surprise Spells](#surprise-spells), [Gold Dragon](#gd-m10) | Arcane Trickster adds sneak attack damage to the spell's damage. This is particularly effective with Gold Dragon; Perfect Soul changes the sneak attack dice to d10s. |
| [Holy Conversion](#holy-conversion), [Negative Energy Conversion](#negative-energy-conversion), [DR and Incorporeal](#dr-and-incorporeal) | Holy or negative energy conversion helps overcome physical damage reduction and immunity. Holy damage also deals full damage to incorporeal enemies, unlike negative energy, which is halved. Conversion can also enable further elemental synergies. |
| [Elemental Conversion](#elemental-conversion), [Fire items](#fire-items), [Elemental Barrage](#elemental-barrage), [Hexes](#std-hexes) | Fire conversion enables *Flame Curse*, which multiplies trap damage by 1.5. It can also trigger *Call of the Fiery Things* and, with a second element, *Elemental Barrage*. |
| [Angel](#mythic-angel), [Lich](#mythic-lich), [Demon](#mythic-demon), [Azata](#mythic-azata), [Devil](#devil-m8), [Gold Dragon](#mythic-gd) | Angel adds damage dice and later converts damage to holy; Lich gets early damage conversion; Demon can reach very high DCs; Azata improves saving throw reliability; Gold Dragon has the highest damage ceiling. |
| [Standard actions](#your-standard-action) | Since traps are free actions, the standard action can be used for dispels, area debuffs, hexes or Dragon Breath.  |

### Video showcase
