<file path="_includes/metamagic.md">
### Metamagic

Metamagic can be applied to Cave Fangs normally, either by taking the relevant feat and adding it to the spellbook, or by using metamagic rods. Quicken and Extend rods can be found throughout the game. The available options are Quicken, Extend, Heighten and Completely Normal (Trickster only).

Only Heighten is useful.

#### Heighten

Heighten increases the DC of the Cave Fangs buff, and the traps inherit that DC. See [DC](#dc).

??? feat "Metamagic (Heighten Spell)"
    {% include '_includes/meta_heighten.md' %}

#### Quicken

Quicken would technically allow three Cave Fangs buffs in one fight: one cast before combat, one as a standard action, and one quickened. A Demon or Aeon could increase this to four. However, more than one buff is never necessary.

??? feat "Metamagic (Quicken Spell)"
    {% include '_includes/meta_quicken.md' %}

#### Forbidden Metamagic 

Four other metamagic feats would be useful that are not on the 'allowed' list for Cave Fangs: Persistent for the saving throws, and Bolster, Empower and Maximise for damage.

??? feat "Metamagic (Persistent Spell)"
    {% include '_includes/meta_persistent.md' %}

??? feat "Metamagic (Bolster Spell)"
    {% include '_includes/meta_bolster.md' %}

??? feat "Metamagic (Empower Spell)"
    {% include '_includes/meta_empower.md' %}

??? feat "Metamagic (Maximize Spell)"
    {% include '_includes/meta_maximize.md' %}

There is no way I know to apply Persistent to Cave Fangs. The other three can be applied through items, Magus Arcana, the Universalist Wizard ability, or [Gold Dragon's Perfect Soul](#gd-m10).

##### Plague of Madness { #meta-plague }

??? item-weapon "Plague of Madness"
    {% include '_includes/plague_of_madness.md' %}

Cast a cantrip three times, then cast the Cave Fangs buff before switching to a more suitable weapon. The traps will be Empowered and Maximised.

##### Promise of Greatness { #meta-greatness}

??? item "Promise of Greatness"
    {% include '_includes/promise_of_greatness.md' %}

This requires Cave Fangs to be cast in combat as the first spell of that combat. This costs a standard action and prevents equipment changes during combat. A weaker encounter can instead be used to activate the buff, leaving plenty of Bolstered traps available for the boss.

??? item "Duality of Conjuration and Summoning"
    {% include '_includes/duality_of_conjuration_and_summoning.md' %}

This book also offers an opportunity. If the 10% chance of the summoned creatures attacking the player occurs, that encounter can be used to cast the buff.

##### Magus Arcana

??? class-feature "Magus Arcana"
    {% include '_includes/magus_arcana.md' %}

Several Magus Arcana allow metamagic to be applied to the next spell. Activating an Arcana grants a permament buff, which remains active until a suitable spell is cast. Activating the Arcana before Cave Fangs: Stalactites applies the metamagic to the buff and the traps are Empowered and Maximised.

??? class-feature "Empowered Magic"
    {% include '_includes/magus_arcana_empowered_magic.md' %}

??? class-feature "Maximized Magic"
    {% include '_includes/magus_arcana_maximized_magic.md' %}

Reach Magic is another useful Metamagic Arcana. It has no effect on the buff itself, but applies to the traps without consuming the charge. Provided no other spells are cast, traps can be placed at a greater distance than normal.

??? class-feature "Reach Magic"
    {% include '_includes/magus_arcana_reach_magic.md' %}

##### Wizard (Universalist)

Universalist Wizards gain Metamagic Mastery at level 8.

It works in much the same way as the Magus Metamagic Arcana.

??? class-feature "Metamagic Mastery"
    {% include '_includes/wizard_metamagic_mastery.md' %}
</file>

<file path="_includes/mobility_stealth.md">
### Avoiding enemy retaliation

Killing all enemies within range is not always sufficient. In spread-out encounters, or fights with multiple phases, some enemies may remain out of reach. Those enemies can then retaliate on the following turn, particularly with ranged attacks.

Two approaches help prevent this: **mobility** and **stealth**.

#### Mobility

When necessary, both the move action and standard action can be used to reposition. Several spells provide substantial movement bonuses:

??? spell "Expeditious Retreat"
    {% include '_includes/expeditious_retreat.md' %}

??? spell "Haste"
    {% include '_includes/haste.md' %}

??? spell "Winds of Vengeance"
    {% include '_includes/winds_of_vengeance.md' %}

An animal companion provides an additional mobility option. It can use both its move action and standard action to move, while its rider retains both actions for combat. This is particularly effective when combined with the movement buffs above.

Classes that have or can gain an animal companion include:

- **Hunter:** 6th-level caster; has Cave Fangs.
- **Sacred Huntmaster:** 6th-level caster.
- **Druid:** 9th-level caster; has Cave Fangs.
- **Oracle with the Nature Mystery:** 9th-level caster; has Cave Fangs.
- **Wildland Shaman:** 9th-level caster; has Cave Fangs.
- **Shaman with the Nature Spirit:** 9th-level caster; has Cave Fangs, but gains the animal companion very late.

Reach Magic can also extend the distance at which traps can be placed.

??? class-feature "Reach Magic"
    {% include '_includes/magus_arcana_reach_magic.md' %}

#### Stealth

In-combat stealth provides another way to avoid retaliation. Once nearby enemies have been killed, there are no remaining enemies in the area to detect the character. Entering stealth prevents distant enemies from moving in to attack. On the following turn, the character can approach and eliminate them.

Several options provide access to in-combat stealth.

**Spells**

Vanish is a common 1st-level spell available to many classes. By this stage, unused 1st-level spell slots are often readily available.

??? spell "Vanish"
    {% include '_includes/vanish.md' %}

Only one round of invisibility is required, so Vanish is sufficient; more expensive spells are unnecessary.

**Class features**

Some classes provide abilities that allow stealth during combat:

- **Witch of the Veil:** Swift-action stealth.

    ??? class-feature "Shrouded Step"
        {% include '_includes/shrouded_step.md' %}

- **Forester and Cult Leader:** Hide in Plain Sight.

    ??? class-feature "Hide in Plain Sight"
        {% include '_includes/hide_in_plain_sight.md' %}

- **Arcane Trickster:** Limited free-action stealth.

    ??? class-feature "Invisible Thief"
        {% include '_includes/invisible_thief.md' %}
</file>

<file path="_includes/mythic_angel.md">
### Angel { #mythic-angel }

#### Mythic 3

At Mythic Rank 3, the Angel unlocks Sword of Heaven, which gives two extra damage dice to spells and abilities. This affects both the traps and Geomancy.

Its duration is very limited at this stage, with only two uses per rest. This allows for its use in two fights per rest.

??? angel-feature "Sword of Heaven"
    {% include '_includes/sword_of_heaven.md' %}

#### Mythic 4

At Mythic Rank 4, the Angel gains access to the first Improved Sword of Heaven upgrade.

Everlasting Flame increases Sword of Heaven's duration to one minute per Mythic Rank. This brings it to three uses per rest, each lasting four minutes.

??? angel-feature "Everlasting Flame"
    {% include '_includes/everlasting_flame.md' %}

#### Mythic 6 { #angel-m6 }

At Mythic Rank 6, the Angel unlocks Sword of Holy Damage, which converts all damage to Holy while Sword of Heaven is active.

Sword of Heaven now has four uses per rest, each lasting six minutes. This is generally sufficient to maintain the effect throughout a dungeon and its encounters.


??? angel-feature "Sword of Holy Damage"
    {% include '_includes/sword_of_holy_damage.md' %}

Around this point, the Storyteller quest also allows the restoration of the Ancient Cloak, rewarding The Bound of Possibility, which increases Sword of Heaven's damage dice by another two.

??? item "The Bound of Possibility"
    {% include '_includes/the_bound_of_possibility.md' %}

Mythic Rank 6 also grants a second Improved Sword of Heaven selection. I recommend Abolish Guile.  

**Abolish Disease/Guile/Poison/Undeath:** Creatures which apply disease/compulsion/poison effect or are undead take 50% more damage.

??? angel-feature "Abolish Sword of Heaven Improvements"
    === "Abolish Disease"
        {%  include '_includes/abolish_disease.md'  %}
        
    === "Abolish Guile"
        {%  include '_includes/abolish_guile.md'  %}
    
    === "Abolish Poison"
        {%  include '_includes/abolish_poison.md'  %}
    
    === "Abolish Undeath"
        {%  include '_includes/abolish_undeath.md'  %}

These bonuses affect all damage, including traps (including the Suprise Spells sneak), Geomancy and Elemental Barrage. 

#### Mythic 7+

Further Improved Sword of Heaven upgrades become available at higher Mythic Ranks.


#### Discussion

The Angel has excellent damage potential at Mythic Rank 6. However, Sword of Heaven has a short duration at earlier ranks, and the lack of damage conversion before Mythic Rank 6 can make incorporeal enemies problematic.

A potential approach is to combine Cave Fangs with a conventional Bolt of Justice Angel build.

??? spell "Bolt of Justice"
    {% include '_includes/bolt_of_justice.md' %}

Bolt of Justice or Storm can handle problematic enemies, while traps deal with the rest. Both easily fit into the same build.

Plague of Madness can also prepare Bolt of Justice, so Bolster is the only essential metamagic feat at the start. (Promise of Greatness, which provides the Bolster effect, is not available until Act 4.)

Once Cave Fangs reaches its full potential, Bolt of Justice can either remain the primary standard-action option, or be replaced by another ability, such as Hymn, a high caster-level Greater Dispel or even a Hex (if conserving resources).

A Wildland Shaman is my prefered class. It can start as a melee-oriented build and then transition into a blaster. Cave Fangs is already on its spell list, and the class provides an animal companion, hexes, and unexpensive access to metamagic feats through the Secret hex choice. Though Flame Curse is not available to this archetype.

??? hex "Secret"
    {% include '_includes/shaman_secret.md' %}

As a Wisdom-based caster, the Shaman has lower potential trap DC than an equivalent Charisma- or Intelligence-based build. However, Bolt of Justice can help address enemies with high Reflex saves.

A two-level dip into Sacred Huntmaster can also be useful. Cunning Initiative adds Wisdom to initiative checks, while the Luck domain provides Bit of Luck. Both abilities help improve initiative reliability.

??? class-feature "Cunning Initiative"
    {% include '_includes/cunning_initiative.md' %}

??? class-feature "Bit of Luck"
    {% include '_includes/bit_of_luck.md' %}

I would not recommend Hell Knight as damage is already high; flexibility, initiative and mobility are more important.
</file>

<file path="_includes/mythic_azata.md">
### Azata { #mythic-azata }

#### Mythic Ranks 4 and 6 { #azata-m4 }

The Azata gains a Superpower at Mythic Ranks 4, 6, 8 and 10. Two Superpowers have particular synergy with Cave Fangs.

Favorable Magic makes enemies less likely to succeed on saving throws. Even when they succeed, the ability increases the damage still dealt.

??? azata-feature "Favorable Magic"
    {% include '_includes/azata_favorable_magic.md' %}

Incredible Might adds damage equal to the Azata's Mythic Rank as a morale bonus. This applies to both Stalactite Traps and Geomancy.

??? azata-feature "Incredible Might" 
    {% include '_includes/azata_incredible_might.md' %}

!!! bug "Ring of Triumphant Advance"
    The Ring of Triumphant Advance does not apply its bonus to non-ray spells.

??? item "Ring of Triumphant Advance"
    {% include '_includes/ring_of_triumphant_advance.md' %}

As a result, the ring does not double the morale damage from Incredible Might.

#### Mythic Rank 8

The Azata gains access to the 6th-level mythic spell Joy of Life at Mythic Rank 8.

??? spell "Joy of Life"
    {% include '_includes/joy_of_life.md' %}

Joy of Life converts the attacks of the Azata and nearby allies to Holy damage. Its duration is only 1 round per 2 caster levels.

#### Mythic Rank 8: Devil { #devil-m8 }

The Azata can transition into the Devil path at Mythic Rank 8. All significant features are retained, with the main trade-off being the loss of the final three Azata levels. The most notable loss is Joy of Life, which would otherwise become available at Mythic Rank 8.

The Devil gains Hell's Decrees.

??? devil-feature "Hell's Decree"
    {% include '_includes/hells_decree.md' %}

    === "Decree of Hunt"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_hunt.md' %}

    === "Decree of Vengeance"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_vengeance.md' %}

    === "Decree of Hell's Fires"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_hells_fires.md' %}

    === "Decree of School Magic"
        {% include '_includes/decree_of_school_magic.md' %}

Decree of Conjuration has synergy with Cave Fangs. 

The decree cannot target the Devil directly. However, it can be reflected back onto the Devil by having an ally wear Razmir's Mask.

??? item "Razmir's Mask"
    {% include '_includes/razmirs_mask.md' %}

This interaction applies to both the Cave Fangs buff and the Stalactite Trap ability, potentially providing up to **+10 DC** at Mythic Rank 10.

#### Mythic Rank 10: Devil

At Mythic Rank 10, the Devil gains Hell's Authority. 

??? devil-feature "Hell's Authority"
    {% include '_includes/devil_hells_authority.md' %}

Aspect of Brimorak would be particularly interesting here. Its additional damage per die also applies to the extra dice from Sword of Heaven. However, the bonus only applies to fire spells, limiting its usefulness with Cave Fangs.

??? demon-feature "Aspect of Brimorak"
    {% include '_includes/aspect_brimorak.md' %}

??? angel-feature "Sword of Heaven"
    {% include '_includes/sword_of_heaven.md' %}

#### Discussion

The main weakness of the Azata is the lack of damage conversion at Mythic Rank 3. Staying on the Azata path provides no conversion until Mythic Rank 8, and Joy of Life only offers a very short duration per cast. 

Incredible Might provides relatively little additional damage without the Ring of Triumphant Advance interaction.
</file>

