### Lich { #mythic-lich }

#### Mythic 3 { #lich-m3 }

At Mythic Rank 3, the Lich can select its first power. For a dedicated Cave Fangs build, the strongest choice is Death of Elements.

??? lich-feature "Death of Elements"
    {% include '_includes/lich_death_of_elements.md' %}

Allowing for immediate geomancy use and bypassing of DR. See [Negative Energy Conversion](#negative-energy-conversion).

Another interesting option is Withering Life.

??? lich-feature "Withering Life"
    {% include '_includes/lich_withering_life.md' %}

This is more relevant to a Primal Regression Lich, where it can act as a free-action enabler, rather than to a dedicated Cave Fangs build. You would still want another option available, for example Stone Call with a Lesser Quicken Rod, against enemies with high Reflex saves.

??? spell "Primal Regression"
    {% include '_includes/primal_regression.md' %}

#### Mythic 5

The Lich gains no specific benefits for Cave Fangs at this rank. However, the [Gloves of Enduring Wizard](#temporary-hit-points) can be found in Act 4, allowing you to take Ascendant Element: Negative without risking self-damage from Geomancy.

#### Mythic 6+

The Lich gains additional powers at Mythic Ranks 6, 8 and 10. None are particularly strong for Cave Fangs.

Fear Control can potentially add some damage to both traps and Geomancy.

??? lich-feature "Fear Control"
    {% include '_includes/lich_fear_control.md' %}

Eclipse Chill only affects Geomancy directly, but its effects can also benefit trap damage. However, it relies on a saving throw that enemies are likely to pass.

??? lich-feature "Eclipse Chill"
    {% include '_includes/lich_eclipse_chill.md' %}

#### Discussion 

The Lich is arguably the best choice for an early, dedicated Cave Fangs build because damage conversion becomes available early. 

The merged spellbook also makes the Lich a natural fit for a full Arcane caster. Since the path has relatively few options for increasing Cave Fangs damage directly, an Arcane Trickster build is an obvious choice. Surprise Spells provides the missing additional damage.

This makes the Lich more specialised around Cave Fangs than the suggested Angel build, which retains Bolt of Justice as an alternative damage option.

The good old solo cheese build: Rowdy Rogue Witch of the Veil, is an obvious candidate, but the playstyle can become extremely tedious. Low BAB and the Witch's limited self-buffing options can make fights, particularly in Act 2, lengthy.

Vivisectionist is a better source of the initial sneak attack dice to meet the Arcane Trickster prerequisite if going for highest cap; see the [Gold Dragon](#mythic-gd)

An Eldritch Scoundrel is another an option I'm exploring. However, its six-level spell progression means an unmerged spellbook and it's got anti-synergy with Arcane Trickster due to it as well.
</file>

<file path="_includes/negative_energy_conversion.md">
### Negative Energy Conversion

The Lich mythic path provides an ability that converts all damage dealt to negative energy.

#### Death of Elements

??? mythic-feature "Death of Elements"
    {% include '_includes/death_of_elements.md' %}

Although the description specifies elemental damage, the ability appears to convert all damage. As with holy conversion, this includes both Geomancy's direct self-damage and its area damage.

The ability becomes available as early as mythic rank 3, considerably earlier than the Angel's equivalent at rank 6.

Since Death Ward grants immunity to negative energy, the Geomancy combination can be used as soon as mythic rank 3. Alternatively Dhampir's have Negative Energy Affinity.

??? spell "Death Ward"
    {% include '_includes/death_ward.md' %}

??? class-feature "Negative Energy Affinity"
    {% include '_includes/racial/trait_negative_energy_affinity.md' %}

#### Downsides compared to (un)holy { #neg-downsides }

- Some enemies, notably undead, are immune to negative energy.
- Damage against incorporeal enemies is still halved, whereas holy damage deals full damage. See [DR and Incorporeal](#dr-and-incorporeal).
- Does not bypass Precision Immunity to Surprise Spells sneak damage.

The first issue can be addressed with Ascendant Element: Negative Energy or the Vulnerability Curse hex. See [Resistances and immunities](#resistances-and-immunities).

The last only by Ascendant Element: Negative Energy.

!!! Warning "Ascendant Element: Negative Energy"
    Ascendant Element: Negative Energy bypasses the caster's own immunity to negative energy, leaving the caster vulnerable to Geomancy self-damage. It is therefore inadvisable before obtaining the [Gloves of Enduring Wizard](#temporary-hit-points).

The following item provides a solution to the first two issues, but only for trap damage.

??? item "Devouring Lust"
    {% include '_includes/devouring_lust.md' %}

The Maximise effect does not work with Cave Fangs, as intended. However, the item also converts negative energy damage to unholy damage without consuming its charges, making this conversion effectively free.

??? note "Conversion from Holy"
    The item also works with damage converted to holy in the previous section, although there is no practical reason to use it for that purpose.

This conversion affects only trap damage, not Geomancy's direct self-damage or area damage. Nor does it affect the Sneak damage from Surprise Spells.

Since Ascendant Element: Negative Energy is inadvisable because of the self-damage, Death of Elements can be toggled off when fighting groups of undead. Vulnerability Curse can then be used against undead bosses with high DR.
</file>

<file path="_includes/smites.md">
### Smites

A smite adds flat damage to the damage rolls of the character using it and bypasses the target's DR. This makes smites a useful, although resource-limited, means of overcoming physical DR. The bonus damage also applies to Geomancy, creating a synergy between the two abilities.

#### The smites

Several smites are available. Apart from the Paladin's Smite Evil, they differ in damage per class level, the level at which they become available, and the number of daily uses.

| Smite | Damage per level | Gained at | Uses per day |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Smite Evil** (Paladin) | 1 | 1 | 1, +1 at level 4 and every 3 levels after (7 at level 19) |
| **Smite Chaos** (Hellknight) | 2 | 1 | 1, +1 at level 4 and every 3 levels after (4 at level 10) |
| **All Is Darkness** (Tortured Crusader) | 1 | 1 | 1, +1 every 5 levels (5 at level 20, not counting 3 more from Unbroken Paragon) |
| **Sworn Enemy - Smite** (Faith Hunter) | 0.5 | 6 | 1, +1 at level 10 and every 4 levels after (4 at level 18) |
| **Smite** (Champion of the Faith) | 1 | 4 | 1, +1 every 4 levels beyond 4th (5 at level 20); consumes 2 uses of Fervor |

??? class-feature "Smites"
    === "Smite Evil"
        {% include '_includes/smite_evil.md' %}

    === "Smite Chaos"
        {% include '_includes/smite_chaos.md' %}

    === "All Is Darkness"
        {% include '_includes/all_is_darkness.md' %}

    === "Sworn Enemy - Smite"
        {% include '_includes/sworn_enemy_smite.md' %}

    === "Smite (Champion of the Faith)"
        {% include '_includes/champion_of_the_faith_smite.md' %}

Two aspects of the descriptions require clarification. The phrase "all damage rolls" refers to damage rolls made by the character who activated the smite. The DR bypass also applies to damage dealt by that character's spells.

#### Synergy with the traps and Geomancy

- Smite bypasses DR for the traps, and for Geomancy when it deals physical damage (Desert, Underground and Urban). See [DR and Incorporeal](#dr-and-incorporeal).
- The bonus damage applies to both the traps and Geomancy.
- When added to trap damage, the bonus is maximised and empowered.
- Other spell damage multipliers also apply to the smite bonus.

#### Extra smites

In addition to the uses gained from class levels, extra uses of Smite Evil are available from:

- The Scroll of Resourceful Smite, from the [Duality of Conjuration and Summoning](#meta-greatness) book.
- The Righteous Crusader's Ring:

??? item "Righteous Crusader's Ring"
    {% include '_includes/righteous_crusaders_ring.md' %}

Both the Paladin and the Hellknight can also gain extra smite uses from mythic abilities:

??? mythic-feature "Abundant Smite"
    {% include '_includes/abundant_smite.md' %}

??? mythic-feature "Abundant Smite Chaos"
    {% include '_includes/abundant_smite_chaos.md' %}

#### Class considerations

The Hellknight is the most level-efficient source of additional damage, making further investment in the class the stronger option for damage scaling.

A single Paladin level can instead provide DR bypass. Combined with the scroll and the ring, this grants three daily uses of Smite Evil: one from the first Paladin level and one from each item.

#### Mark of Justice

The Paladin also gains Mark of Justice, which grants all allies the ability to smite evil for 1 minute, using the Paladin's bonuses. An allied Paladin can therefore provide these bonuses without requiring any Paladin levels in the main build.

??? class-feature "Mark of Justice"
    {% include '_includes/mark_of_justice.md' %}
</file>

<file path="_includes/standard_action.md">
Cave Fangs traps are placed as a free action, leaving the standard action available for other abilities. The following options are not exhaustive.

### Dispels

In addition to removing buffs that might increase saving throws or reduce damage, dispels can lower enemy saving throws through the following effects:

- **Dispel Synergy:** A successful dispel applies a –2 penalty to the target's saving throws.
- **Destructive Dispel:** A successful targeted dispel can leave the target stunned or sickened. The sickened condition applies a further –2 penalty to saving throws.

??? feat "Dispel Synergy"
    {% include '_includes/dispel_synergy.md' %}

??? feat "Destructive Dispel"
    {% include '_includes/destructive_dispel.md' %}

Greater Dispel Magic is the readily available spell. 

 It's especially effective with mostly mono-class Angel or Lich builds, which can reach high caster levels through their merged spellbooks.

??? spell "Dispel Magic, Greater"
    {% include '_includes/greater_dispel_magic.md' %}

Lich also has a particularly powerful dispel that can severely reduce the saving throws of heavily buffed enemies, making it an effective boss-killing tool.

??? spell "Corrupt Magic"
    {% include '_includes/corrupt_magic.md' %}

The Gold Dragon can also dispel effects through an upgrade to Dragon Breath. 

??? gd-feature "Mind-Clearing Breath"
    {% include '_includes/gold_dragon_mind_clearing_breath.md' %}

### Area debuffs

Angel and Demon each have a mythic spell that applies an area-wide saving throw penalty without allowing a saving throw.

??? spell "Profane Hymn"
    {% include '_includes/profane_hymn.md' %}

??? spell "Holy Hymn"
    {% include '_includes/holy_hymn.md' %}

The Gold Dragon has the aforementioned Dragon Breath ability.

??? gd-feature "Dragon Breath"
    {% include '_includes/gold_dragon_gold_dragon_breath.md' %}

### Hexes { #std-hexes }

Hexes are free standard actions and therefore pair well with Cave Fangs.

Evil Eye reduces enemy saving throws.

??? hex "Evil Eye"
    {% include '_includes/evil_eye.md' %}

Flame Curse multiplies trap damage by 1.5 when fire conversion is active.

??? hex "Flame Curse"
    {% include '_includes/flame_curse.md' %}

Vulnerability Curse allows elemental and negative energy immunities to be bypassed on a single target. This can enable Elemental Barrage without substantial mythic investment, or allow Death of Elements plus Geomancy to be used without the risk of [self-sabotage](#neg-downsides).

??? hex "Vulnerability Curse"
    {% include '_includes/vulnerability_curse.md' %}
</file>

<file path="_includes/surprise_spells.md">
### Surprise Spells

??? class-feature "Surprise Spells"
    {% include '_includes/surprise_spells.md' %}

Surprise Spells adds sneak attack damage to all your traps, which is a very significant damage boost.

There are some limits:

- The damage is of the same type as the spell, but it is still sneak (precision) damage. Enemies that are immune to precision damage are immune to it. Can be bypassed by [holy conversion](#holy-conversion) or [negative energy conversion](#negative-energy-conversion) *with* Ascendant Element: Negative.
- The damage is not maximized or empowered along with the traps.
- With double conversion, for example holy or negative energy converted on to fire, the sneak damage stays as the first type, while the damage of the traps is converted onwards to fire.
- Geomancy does not get the sneak attack damage.
- The usual sneak attack synergy (such as Petrifying Strike) require an attack roll to work. 

#### Uncanny Dodge

Enemies with Uncanny Dodge cannot be caught flat-footed. The Arcane Trickster's Impromptu Sneak Attack helps with this, as the target loses its Dexterity bonus to AC against the attacks.

Alter Ego will do the same but much more economically, but requires three level of Assassin.

??? class-feature "Uncanny Dodge"
    {% include '_includes/uncanny_dodge.md' %}

??? class-feature "Impromptu Sneak Attack"
    {% include '_includes/impromptu_sneak_attack.md' %}

??? class-feature "Alter Ego"
    {% include '_includes/alter_ego.md' %}


#### Arcane Trickster 

Surprise Spells comes from the Arcane Trickster prestige class. The prerequisites are:

| Requirement | |
|---|---|
| Skills | Mobility 4, Trickery 4, Knowledge (Arcana) 4 |
| Sneak attack | 2d6 |
| Spells | Arcane spells of 2nd level |

The recommended route is 1 level of Rowdy, then 3 levels of Wizard or Witch (taking the Accomplished Sneak Attacker feat at character level 3), then 10 levels of Arcane Trickster.

??? feat "Accomplished Sneak Attacker"
    {% include '_includes/accomplished_sneak_attacker.md' %}
</file>

